claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> This summarizes a terminal session with Pat about a failure in the
> "[PATCH v3 0/6] Add just process-email" series [1], including
> "[PATCH v3 7/6] libexec/mail: Leave published work in place" [2]. It
> records the diagnosis and the agreed direction, and asks the questions
> I need answered before sending patches.
>
> The failure
> -----------
>
> Pat ran "just process-email" from ~/lab/default.jj with @ on top of
> the series. The pass answered the signoz thread and pushed the reply,
> and the message left the inbox. The loop then died:
>
>     error: justfile does not contain recipe `notify`
>
> The cause is that the driver runs from the tree that the agent edits.
> During its pass the agent ran "jj new b/dev@upstream" and then "jj new
> trunk@upstream" in the default workspace, as the process-email skill
> tells it to. It then built "[PATCH 0/2] Build ports without building
> their dependencies" [3] there. The recipe body had already been loaded,
> but each nested "just notify" and "just get-up-to-date" reads the
> Justfile from disk again. By then @ was no longer on the series, so
> the notify recipe was gone. The next iteration would have failed the
> same way at get-up-to-date.
>
> A second, separate bug turned up while reading get-up-to-date.sh. It
> rebases with "-s roots(WORK) -d trunk@upstream", where WORK is limited
> to mine(). If my series sits on a list patch with another author,
> applied with "just am", that patch is not in WORK. My series is then
> moved onto trunk and loses the prerequisite it needs.
>
> Options considered
> ------------------
>
> 1. Run each agent pass in its own jj workspace, so the agent never
>    moves the driver's working copy.
> 2. Move the driver out of the tree, so it cannot change its own files
>    while running.
>
> I suggested installing the existing just recipes and libexec helpers
> out of tree. Pat rejected that in favor of a cleaner split.
>
> Agreed direction
> ----------------
>
> - A new tool, src/lab-process-mail, does everything the loop does,
>   with no just involved: get up to date, pick the message, call
>   claude with a prompt for the part that needs judgement, check the
>   outcome from VCS and notmuch state, and notify.
> - A "just install" task packages it as a FreeBSD package and
>   installs it as the user, the way dotfiles/emacs/Justfile and
>   patmaddox-emacs.pkg.do do for the Emacs configuration. The
>   installed copy is what runs, so a pass can never edit the driver
>   while it runs.
>
> Questions before I send patches
> -------------------------------
>
> 1. Install path. You wrote ~/local/bin. Neither ~/local/bin nor
>    ~/.local/bin exists in my jail, and neither is on PATH. Which one
>    should it be? I would use a package prefix of /local and install
>    with "pkg -r ~", as the Emacs package does.

I want to roughly mirror how upstream pkg installation works, and
ultimately this stuff may get contributed to ports. So really I'm
installing to LOCALBASE and I guess that shouldn't be baked into the
package - rather it is `pkg -r ~/local` maybe? So ultimately it will be
at ~/local/bin/lab-process-email.

It's not on PATH yet but we'll add it. In fact make a patch for
lab-claude that adds it for claude user.

> 2. Where does the prompt live? If the tool calls claude with
>    "/process-email <id>", the procedure still comes from
>    .claude/skills/process-email in whatever tree claude starts in,
>    and an agent can change that file. The alternative is to ship
>    the prompt in the package, e.g. share/lab-process-mail/, and pass
>    it to "claude -p", so the whole loop is pinned at install time.
>    I lean toward shipping it in the package. Should the in-tree
>    skill then go away, or stay for interactive use?

Yes the prompt will be co-located with the script. We are extracting the
logic out of the skill. Ultimately there will be no claude skill for
this - I don't need the skill behavior. What I need is a deterministic
tool that prompts an agent for the non-deterministic work.

> 3. Workspaces. Out of tree, the driver cannot break itself, but the
>    agent still moves default@ under you. Should lab-process-mail
>    also give the agent its own workspace, e.g. ~/lab/work.jj created
>    with "jj workspace add" and reset before each pass?

Yeah I think that's a good idea. That or the prompt tells claude to use
workspaces, I know it has some of that behavior built in. I'm open to
either - whichever will be more effective.

Ultimately what I want is for the claude that kicks off to be an
autonomous developer, so I don't want to be overly prescriptive.

What I'm designing here is an work queue, and claude should develop
lab. So I'm open to design suggestions on how to accomplish that.

> 4. Fate of v3. I read "no just involved" as replacing "just
>    process-email", "just get-up-to-date", "just notify", and
>    get-up-to-date.sh, so I would withdraw v3 and post a new series
>    against trunk. Some of v3 is independent of the driver, such as
>    the skill taking the message id as an argument. Should that part
>    go in the new series, or be dropped?

Yes exactly. We're taking the ideas in this patch series and formalizing
the implementation a bit more, to be a structured tool rather than an
unstructured skill. So the changes like it doesn't loop, it takes a
specific message, etc that needs to carry through.

Essentially we're just extracting this functionality to a dedicated
tool.

> 5. The rebase bug above. Should I fix it in the new tool, by
>    rebasing whole stacks with "-b" or by leaving stacks with
>    non-mine prerequisites in place, or send it as a separate patch?

Yes fix the bug in the tool, but do it as a separate commit. So I'd like
to extract the logic mostly as is, bug and all, and have a commit that
clearly fixes it.

> 6. Language and tests. I would write it in sh(1) with kyua tests,
>    like src/sclr. Notifications would go to NOTIFY_URL, as in v3, so
>    no topic is baked into the package. Is that right?

That's right.
